1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,238
In a pairs game, one child is left alone.

2
00:00:03,238 --> 00:00:05,239
A cricket team plays with ten.

3
00:00:05,239 --> 00:00:07,724
A big lift carries twelve, not eight.

4
00:00:08,124 --> 00:00:11,045
Each time, a rule that
works most days breaks.

5
00:00:11,045 --> 00:00:12,831
Chemistry has one like that.

6
00:00:13,281 --> 00:00:15,208
Aarav is revising late at night.

7
00:00:15,208 --> 00:00:18,399
In BF three, boron has only
six electrons around it.

8
00:00:18,749 --> 00:00:21,435
But the octet rule said
every atom wants eight.

9
00:00:21,435 --> 00:00:22,804
So is the rule wrong?

10
00:00:23,304 --> 00:00:27,812
By the end, you will know exactly
where the octet rule breaks, and why.

11
00:00:28,462 --> 00:00:30,018
First, the rule itself.

12
00:00:30,368 --> 00:00:32,111
Think of a sticker album.

13
00:00:32,111 --> 00:00:35,499
A page feels complete only
when every slot is filled.

14
00:00:35,849 --> 00:00:37,657
An atom's outer shell is that page.

15
00:00:37,657 --> 00:00:38,992
It is the valence shell.

16
00:00:38,992 --> 00:00:40,784
Its electrons are valence electrons.

17
00:00:41,134 --> 00:00:45,354
The octet rule says atoms join so each
ends with eight valence electrons.

18
00:00:45,354 --> 00:00:47,211
Octet means a group of eight.

19
00:00:47,561 --> 00:00:50,630
Neon already has eight,
so it does not react.

20
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:53,650
The rule was built on
these quiet noble gases.

21
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:56,784
Let's test the rule on our molecule bench.

22
00:00:56,784 --> 00:01:00,180
Boron sits in the middle with
only three valence electrons.

23
00:01:00,530 --> 00:01:02,526
Three chlorine atoms come in.

24
00:01:02,526 --> 00:01:06,676
Each shares one electron with
boron, and boron shares one back.

25
00:01:07,076 --> 00:01:09,002
Now count the electrons around boron.

26
00:01:09,002 --> 00:01:10,083
Two, four, six.

27
00:01:10,083 --> 00:01:10,983
Only six!

28
00:01:11,125 --> 00:01:13,064
Each chlorine gets its eight.

29
00:01:13,064 --> 00:01:14,460
Boron stops at six.

30
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:16,351
This is an incomplete octet.

31
00:01:16,751 --> 00:01:21,025
Lithium, beryllium and boron have just
one, two and three valence electrons.

32
00:01:21,025 --> 00:01:24,104
So Li Cl, Be H two and B
Cl three all fall short.

33
00:01:24,754 --> 00:01:26,091
The second break.

34
00:01:26,091 --> 00:01:27,647
Nitric oxide, NO.

35
00:01:27,647 --> 00:01:31,113
Nitrogen brings five valence
electrons, oxygen six.

36
00:01:31,463 --> 00:01:33,139
Five plus six is eleven.

37
00:01:33,139 --> 00:01:35,495
Electrons sit in pairs, like socks.

38
00:01:35,495 --> 00:01:37,411
Eleven socks leave one alone.

39
00:01:37,811 --> 00:01:40,194
So one atom stays short of eight.

40
00:01:40,194 --> 00:01:41,889
NO two has seventeen.

41
00:01:41,889 --> 00:01:42,789
Odd again.

42
00:01:43,183 --> 00:01:44,396
Pause and think.

43
00:01:44,396 --> 00:01:46,930
Which one is an odd electron molecule?

44
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:48,567
B Cl three, or NO?

45
00:01:51,767 --> 00:01:52,667
NO!

46
00:01:52,325 --> 00:01:54,036
Five plus six is eleven.

47
00:01:54,036 --> 00:01:58,211
B Cl three has twenty four, even,
but boron still falls short.

48
00:01:58,861 --> 00:01:59,761
Third break.

49
00:01:59,683 --> 00:02:01,556
Some atoms hold more than eight.

50
00:02:01,906 --> 00:02:02,806
Step one.

51
00:02:02,717 --> 00:02:07,044
In PF five, phosphorus makes five
bonds with five fluorine atoms.

52
00:02:07,794 --> 00:02:08,694
Step two.

53
00:02:08,535 --> 00:02:11,182
Each bond is a shared pair, two electrons.

54
00:02:11,182 --> 00:02:13,595
Five times two is ten around phosphorus.

55
00:02:14,345 --> 00:02:15,283
Step three.

56
00:02:15,283 --> 00:02:17,967
In SF six, sulphur makes six bonds.

57
00:02:17,967 --> 00:02:19,752
Six times two is twelve.

58
00:02:20,502 --> 00:02:21,402
Why can they?

59
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,601
Phosphorus and sulphur
are in period three.

60
00:02:23,601 --> 00:02:26,151
Besides 3 s and 3 p,
they have 3 d orbitals.

61
00:02:26,151 --> 00:02:27,899
Orbitals are rooms for electrons.

62
00:02:27,899 --> 00:02:29,440
More rooms, more electrons.

63
00:02:30,190 --> 00:02:32,114
This is the expanded octet.

64
00:02:32,114 --> 00:02:35,354
H two SO four also puts
twelve around sulphur.

65
00:02:36,104 --> 00:02:38,381
But sulphur can also follow the rule.

66
00:02:38,381 --> 00:02:40,667
In S Cl two, it has exactly eight.

67
00:02:41,317 --> 00:02:42,320
Remember this.

68
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,496
The octet rule is a useful guide.

69
00:02:44,496 --> 00:02:46,343
It is not a law of nature.

70
00:02:47,043 --> 00:02:48,733
Now, one worked example.

71
00:02:49,033 --> 00:02:53,135
Count the electrons around the central
atom in Be Cl two, P Cl five and

72
00:02:53,135 --> 00:02:54,838
S Cl two, and sort each.

73
00:02:55,338 --> 00:02:56,288
Be Cl two.

74
00:02:56,288 --> 00:02:58,759
Two bonds, two times two is four.

75
00:02:58,759 --> 00:03:00,004
Incomplete octet.

76
00:03:00,754 --> 00:03:01,791
P Cl five.

77
00:03:01,791 --> 00:03:04,547
Five bonds, five times two is ten.

78
00:03:04,547 --> 00:03:05,796
Expanded octet.

79
00:03:06,546 --> 00:03:07,446
S Cl two.

80
00:03:07,401 --> 00:03:08,848
Two bonds make four.

81
00:03:08,848 --> 00:03:12,696
Sulphur also keeps two lone
pairs, pairs it does not share.

82
00:03:12,696 --> 00:03:13,842
That adds four.

83
00:03:14,592 --> 00:03:16,130
Four plus four is eight.

84
00:03:16,130 --> 00:03:18,222
So S Cl two obeys the octet rule.

85
00:03:18,872 --> 00:03:21,433
The octet rule has three more weak spots.

86
00:03:21,783 --> 00:03:22,683
One.

87
00:03:22,376 --> 00:03:25,122
It assumes noble gases never react.

88
00:03:25,122 --> 00:03:30,579
But xenon and krypton form X e
F two, K r F two and X e OF two.

89
00:03:30,979 --> 00:03:31,879
Two.

90
00:03:31,399 --> 00:03:33,605
It cannot tell the shape of a molecule.

91
00:03:34,005 --> 00:03:34,905
Three.

92
00:03:34,565 --> 00:03:38,635
It says nothing about energy, so it
cannot tell which molecule is more

93
00:03:38,635 --> 00:03:39,535
stable.

94
00:03:39,892 --> 00:03:41,354
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:03:41,704 --> 00:03:46,077
Some students think nitrogen can expand
like phosphorus, so N Cl five should

96
00:03:46,077 --> 00:03:46,977
exist.

97
00:03:47,041 --> 00:03:48,982
But nitrogen is in period two.

98
00:03:48,982 --> 00:03:52,690
It has no 2 d orbitals, so it
never holds more than eight.

99
00:03:52,690 --> 00:03:53,923
P Cl five exists.

100
00:03:53,923 --> 00:03:55,303
N Cl five does not.

101
00:03:55,953 --> 00:03:57,305
Where is this asked?

102
00:03:57,305 --> 00:04:01,549
As odd one out questions: pick the
molecule that breaks the octet rule.

103
00:04:02,149 --> 00:04:03,307
Exam level now.

104
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:06,790
In which set does every
molecule break the octet rule?

105
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:07,996
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:13,396 --> 00:04:14,823
The answer is A.

107
00:04:14,823 --> 00:04:17,355
BF three, NO and PF five.

108
00:04:17,855 --> 00:04:20,774
In BF three, boron has only six.

109
00:04:20,774 --> 00:04:21,787
Incomplete.

110
00:04:22,287 --> 00:04:24,570
NO has eleven electrons, odd.

111
00:04:24,570 --> 00:04:27,069
PF five has ten around phosphorus.

112
00:04:27,069 --> 00:04:27,969
Expanded.

113
00:04:28,354 --> 00:04:32,921
Every other set hides one molecule
that obeys the rule: NH three, CO

114
00:04:32,921 --> 00:04:33,982
two or water.

115
00:04:34,582 --> 00:04:36,122
Here is your recap.

116
00:04:36,122 --> 00:04:37,705
Screenshot this card.

117
00:04:40,905 --> 00:04:43,091
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:04:43,491 --> 00:04:44,391
One.

119
00:04:43,872 --> 00:04:46,534
Why does boron have only
six electrons in B Cl three?

120
00:04:47,334 --> 00:04:48,234
Two.

121
00:04:47,791 --> 00:04:51,032
Why can no structure give
every atom in NO an octet?

122
00:04:51,832 --> 00:04:52,732
Three.

123
00:04:52,394 --> 00:04:55,634
Why does P Cl five exist,
but N Cl five does not?

124
00:04:56,534 --> 00:05:00,735
Next time: why is a triple bond shorter
and stronger than a single bond?

125
00:05:00,735 --> 00:05:02,629
We find out in bond parameters.
